it's been a hot and dry summer and  unusually some parts of the uk haven't  had rain in weeks and that's led to  looming fears of drought  drought is essentially a prolonged dry  period that has caused a water shortage  every drought is different depending on  where it is what time of year it is and  the type of environment  this makes it difficult to pin down  exactly when one officially begins  but essentially it's when a lack of  water starts to have a serious effect on  our lives  it can be really damaging in the u.s  drought is the second most costly  weather event after hurricanes there are  four stages normal prolonged dry weather  drought and severe drought with england  currently sitting in the second  prolonged dry weather one  and there are various causes of droughts  including changing temperatures and  weather patterns a higher demand for  water and soil degradation  you have three main types of drought  environmental agricultural and water  supply  environmental drought is when a shortage  of rainfall has an impact on the  environment this could affect the land  wildlife and fish  agricultural drought occurs when there  isn't enough rain to support crop  production and other farming practices  and a water supply drought also known as  a hydrological drought happens when the  dry spell is long enough to seriously  impact water supply this means rivers  reservoirs or groundwater supplies dry  up and is very bad news  [Music]  droughts have happened throughout  mankind's history  so are nothing new  but one of the main causes of drought is  a changing temperature and that is  something we are seeing more and more of  thanks to climate change  climate change is making the weather  more extreme the drier place is drier  and the wetter place is wetter so in  warmer places the water in the land  dries up quicker than it used to  and less water in the soil means more  drought  there are some ways we can try and stop  droughts becoming so common  before the drought starts we can  implement things like hosepipe bans  commercial and residential water  restrictions and we can try to manage  our water more carefully generally we  can also try to recycle water more water  from home is usually sent straight to  the sewer but water from baths showers  washing machines dishwashers and sinks  is known as grey water and is much  easier to recycle  it is estimated this could save 70  liters of water per person per day in  domestic households  on a bigger scale we can also try to  fight climate change preserve more water  improve the water infrastructure around  the world and make our water management  generally much better  but we can't control the weather and  with the world getting hotter droughts  are sadly going to become more and more  common
 

